**What was chosen, and why.** The brief's first option (a Lean gap from items 06–09) does not exist. The second, Fomin–Høie's Lemma 4 in Lean, was priced above one sorry-free session after reading its proof: it needs path decompositions with a prescribed last bag, a "fewer edges than vertices ⇒ acyclic component" lemma that Mathlib lacks, the tree bound transported to a component, and a three-piece pasting. So the session did the non-Lean half of option 2 and all of option 3.

**Done**
- **Lean, option 3.** `lean/MOSPFormalization/DegreeBound/StepBound.lean`: 9 theorems, no `sorry`, no `axiom`, built on the first attempt. R6 (`SPStep.pathwidth_le`, one operation costs at most one stack) and the unconditional bound along a whole reduction, `pw G ≤ pw H + |supp G ∖ supp H|`, via the new subgraph fact `G − (supp G ∖ supp H) ≤ H`. The file, report and a test all say this is not the reduction lemma (H3): its budget is one per removed vertex.
- **Lemma 4 checked exactly** on every max-degree-3 graph to 12 vertices and every end bag `X` (175,574,074 pairs), using an exact end-bag pathwidth DP whose derivation is in report §11.2. Zero violations of the lemma as printed, as its proof gives it, and of a Lean-ready integer form. The term the record needs is at most 1 at every size, against the printed `(2/3)·log₃ n + 1`.
- **A finding about the printed proof.** Case 3.A applies Theorem 3 to a tree on at most `2n/3` vertices and writes `(2/3)·log₃ n`, but Theorem 3 gives `log₃(2n/3)`, which is below `(2/3)·log₃ n` only for `n ≤ 3`. The proof as printed establishes the lemma with `log₃ n`. No consequence for Theorem 5, (H1) or the paper; the stated transfer lemma in report §4.3 is corrected in place. Whether the statement with `(2/3)·log₃ n` holds in general is open.
- **(H3)'s shape at 10 vertices**: on a 9.6% deterministic slice of the 10-vertex graphs (1,154,239 graphs, not a uniform sample), `pw(G) − pw(r(G))` is still at most 3.
- Bookkeeping: 9 axiom lines (586 theorems, 0 failures), sandwich names folded in, three new tests (55 in all), report §11 plus §0.3, §0.6 and §4.3 updates, PROGRESS entry, item marked `[x]`, `LOOP_COMPLETE` appended.

